TEXT: Article L-ARLPT: An LLM-Augmented Reinforcement Learning Framework for Autonomous Penetration Testing Rufeng Zhan 1 , Junyi Zhu 2,3, Yinghui Xu 1, Chan Chen 1 , Rongxin Hu 1 and Le Wang 1,* 1 Cyberspace Institute of Advanced Technology, Guangzhou University, Guangzhou 510700, China; 2006400027@e.gzhu.edu.cn (R.Z.); xuyinghui@e.gzhu.edu.cn (Y.X.); chenchan01@e.gzhu.edu.cn (C.C.); hurongxin@e.gzhu.edu.cn (R.H.) 2 Department of Computer Science and Engineering, Southern University of Science and Technology, Shenzhen 518055, China; 12547014@mail.sustech.edu.cn 3 Department of New Networks, Peng Cheng Laboratory, Xili Street, Shenzhen 518000, China * Correspondence: wangle@gzhu.edu.cn Abstract In recent years, Deep Reinforcement Learning (DRL) has emerged as a promising approach for automating penetration testing due to its capability to perform sequential decisionmaking in complex environments. However, in real-world enterprise networks, attack actions are typically characterized by highly coupled multi-dimensional parameter combinations, resulting in an exponentially expanding discrete action space. Such a large action space significantly degrades exploration efficiency and prevents conventional DRL agents from learning effective attack paths under sparse-reward conditions. To address these challenges, this paper proposes a Large Language Model-enhanced Autonomous Reinforcement Learning Penetration Testing framework (L-ARLPT). Specifically, the framework leverages the domain knowledge embedded in a Large Language Model (LLM) to perform tactical planning, thereby pruning the original action space into a compact set of candidate actions. Subsequently, an experience-driven layer employs the optimization mechanism of a Deep Q-Network (DQN) to conduct value estimation and policy learning within the reduced candidate set. To validate the effectiveness of the proposed framework, a high-fidelity enterprise penetration-testing simulation environment was constructed based on realistic enterprise attack scenarios. Experimental results demonstrate that, in a high-fidelity enterprise penetration-testing environment with a raw theoretical parametercombination space containing 6 × 107 combinations, the proposed L-ARLPT framework achieves an average penetration depth of 3.33 out of 4.00, substantially outperforming both reinforcement learning baselines (all ≤1.27) and LLM-based baselines (1.12). Moreover, successful episodes require only 128.43 decision steps on average, enabling long-horizon, cross-domain lateral penetration in high-dimensional discrete action spaces. Academic Editor: George Drosatos Received: 11 June 2026 Revised: 5 August 2026 Accepted: 6 August 2026 Published: 7 August 2026 Copyright: © 2026 by the authors. Licensee MDPI, Basel, Switzerland. This article is an open access article distributed under the terms and conditions of the Creative Commons Attribution (CC BY) license. Keywords: automated penetration testing; reinforcement learning; large language model; prompt engineering; action space pruning 1. Introduction As cybersecurity threats continue to evolve, red teaming has become an important approach for evaluating the effectiveness of defensive systems [1]. However, its strong dependence on human expertise incurs substantial labor and time costs, limiting scalability in practice [2,3]. To address this limitation, automated penetration testing has attracted increasing attention. Among existing approaches, DRL is particularly promising Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 https://doi.org/10.3390/app16167887 Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 2 of 27 because it can learn complex attack strategies through continuous interaction with the environment [4–6]. The action space in real-world penetration testing environments often grows exponentially, and handling large action spaces has been identified as one of the core challenges faced by autonomous penetration testing agents [7,8]. Moreover, strict prerequisite dependencies among attack stages lead to sparse effective feedback, further increasing the difficulty of reinforcement learning-based attack planning [9]. To address the exploration inefficiency caused by high-dimensional combinatorial action spaces, existing studies can be broadly categorized into four research directions. The first category addresses large discrete action spaces through conftinuous action representations. Representative studies employ the Wolpertinger architecture [10,11], which maps discrete attack actions into a continuous embedding space and retrieves candidate actions via nearest-neighbor search. More recently, APRIL further improves scalability by learning transferable semantic action embeddings, enabling efficient policy transfer across different penetration-testing environments while alleviating the optimization difficulty caused by large discrete action spaces [12]. Although such methods improve scalability in large action spaces, the additional retrieval process may reduce policy optimization efficiency. The second category focuses on action decomposition. Representative approaches include the hierarchical CRLA framework [13] and dimension-wise decomposition methods such as Action Branching [14] and ABQ [15], and more recent hierarchical penetrationtesting frameworks based on action clipping and decomposition [16], which further reduce exploration complexity by combining action decomposition with reward-guided action pruning. By decomposing combinatorial action spaces into smaller decision units, these methods reduce search complexity. However, they often face challenges in modeling inter-action dependencies and maintaining semantic consistency among decomposed action components. The third category attempts to filter infeasible actions using prior knowledge or auxiliary models. Representative studies employ MulVAL-based attack dependency graphs [17–19] to pre-screen candidate actions. While these approaches reduce ineffective exploration to some extent, they generally rely on static rules or simple models and therefore exhibit limited adaptability to dynamic environments and complex attack scenarios. The fourth category improves the exploration mechanism of reinforcement learning agents. For instance, DUSC-DQN [20] combines historical information with upper-confidencebound exploration to improve sample efficiency. Nevertheless, such methods do not fundamentally reduce the size of the action space and therefore remain computationally expensive in large combinatorial environments. In summary, existing studies have improved exploration efficiency through action representation learning, action decomposition, rule-based constraints, and exploration-strategy optimization. However, most approaches still rely heavily on trial-and-error [21,22] learning and cannot effectively exploit cybersecurity domain knowledge to fundamentally reduce the search space. More recently, researchers have begun exploiting the prior knowledge embedded in LLMs to guide reinforcement learning for automated penetration testing. Representative approaches employ LLM-generated action constraints or masking rules to reduce ineffective exploration and improve training efficiency [21]. While existing methods typically use offline, fixed LLMderived constraints, our framework performs step-wise online semantic reasoning. At each decision step, the LLM analyzes the current context (including new observations and historical feedback) and dynamically reconstructs an executable candidate action set. Rather than relying on precomputed rules, we continuously incorporate environmental feedback into subsequent reasoning cycles via contextual memory. Consequently, efficiently integrating domain knowledge with reinforcement learning remains an open challenge [23] in realistic https://doi.org/10.3390/app16167887 Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 3 of 27 penetration-testing environments characterized by high-dimensional combinatorial action spaces, strict prerequisite dependencies, and sparse rewards. Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have opened new opportunities for automated penetration testing by leveraging extensive domain knowledge and powerful reasoning capabilities [24]. Nevertheless, their reliance on static pretraining corpora makes them vulnerable to factual hallucinations, while the absence of an environment-interactiondriven value optimization mechanism limits their ability to adapt to dynamically evolving environments and maximize long-term attack effectiveness [25,26]. Motivated by these observations, this work integrates the reasoning capability of LLMs with the long-term value optimization ability of DRL and proposes L-ARLPT, a framework for autonomous penetration testing in high-dimensional action spaces. The main contributions of this work are summarized as follows: • We propose L-ARLPT, an LLM-augmented reinforcement learning framework designed for autonomous penetration testing in large combinatorial action spaces. The framework employs a knowledge-driven layer to perform two-stage semantic action-space pruning based on cybersecurity knowledge and attack-dependency constraints, dynamically reducing the original action space to a small set of high-quality candidate actions. An experience-driven layer then performs value estimation and policy learning within the candidate set using DQN, reducing ineffective exploration while improving long-horizon attack-path discovery. • We design a dynamic memory management mechanism with separated positive and negative feedback. To address the tendency of LLMs to become trapped in repetitive trial-and-error loops during multi-step decision-making, the proposed mechanism independently manages sliding-window negative feedback and global positive feedback, enabling online knowledge correction and hallucination suppression while reducing computational resources wasted on ineffective attempts. • We construct a controlled enterprise penetration-testing simulation platform comprising ten representative business nodes distributed across four logical security zones. Following realistic enterprise network architectures, red-team tactics such as reconnaissance, credential theft, privilege escalation, and cross-domain lateral movement are formalized as POMDP tasks with strict causal dependencies. Although compact in node count, the platform preserves the sparse-reward characteristics, multi-stage prerequisite dependencies, and long-horizon decision-making challenges targeted in this study, thereby providing a standardized and reproducible benchmark for evaluating intelligent attack agents in high-dimensional action spaces. The remainder of this paper is organized as follows. Section 2 formulates the penetration testing problem as a POMDP and describes the simulation environment. Section 3 presents the proposed L-ARLPT framework. Section 4 reports the experimental results and ablation studies. Section 5 concludes the paper and discusses future work. 2. Simulation Environment Design and Problem Formulation This study constructs a penetration-testing simulation environment based on a realistic enterprise network and formulates it as a Partially Observable Markov Decision Process (POMDP) [27] with multiple isolated security domains. 2.1. Problem Formulation In automated penetration testing, attackers generally have no prior knowledge of the complete network configuration and must perform sequential decision-making based solely on limited local observations. To characterize this process, the automated penetration https://doi.org/10.3390/app16167887 Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 4 of 27 testing task is formalized as a POMDP defined by the six-tuple (S, A, T, R, O, Ω), where each component is specified as follows. 1. State Space (S): The global state s ∈ S encodes both the system attributes and asset attributes of all network nodes. System attributes include operating systems, open ports, running services, and existing vulnerabilities, whereas asset attributes include user privileges, credentials, sensitive files, and proxy tunnels. 2. Action Space (A): Each attack action is represented as a parameterized combination of multiple components, such as attack type, target entity, credentials, and tools. The Cartesian-product formulation results in a large discrete action space with complex dependencies among action components. 3. State Transition Function (T): State transitions in the proposed environment are deterministic. If an action a satisfies the prerequisite conditions under state s, such as network connectivity, access privileges, and credential availability, the environment transitions to a new state with probability 1. Otherwise, the action fails and the state remains unchanged. 4. Reward Function (R): The reward function provides an immediate scalar feedback signal to the agent when a state transition occurs. Since only a small fraction of actions are effective within the high-dimensional action space, the resulting reward signal is highly sparse. 5. Observation Space (Ω) and Observation Function (O): The agent can only access partial observations. An observation ot ∈ Ω consists of information obtained through previous attack actions, including discovered system attributes and asset attributes. Therefore, the observation function can be formulated as: ot = O(st, Ht) (1) where st denotes the complete environment state and Ht represents the historical interaction trajectory available to the agent. The observation function extracts only the currently discovered information from the hidden global state rather than exposing the complete state representation. As attack actions reveal additional resources, vulnerabilities, and credentials, the observation evolves over time, enabling the agent to gradually increase its knowledge about the environment. In the current implementation, state transitions are deterministic to support controlled and reproducible evaluation of predefined penetration-testing scenarios. Real enterprise networks may exhibit stochasticity due to network latency, service instability, defensive mechanisms, and concurrent activities; extending the framework to stochastic transition models is therefore an important direction for future work. Overall, the proposed POMDP captures the high-dimensional combinatorial action space, partial observability, and sparse feedback characteristics of automated penetration testing. 2.2. Simulation Environment Design Real-world penetration testing environments are characterized by uncontrollable state transitions and the absence of standardized interaction interfaces, making them unsuitable for the iterative training and evaluation of reinforcement learning algorithms. To address these limitations, a red-team internal network penetration simulation platform was developed. The platform is designed to provide a controlled and reproducible evaluation environment that instantiates the POMDP model defined in Section 2.1. By reproducing realistic attack dependencies and long-horizon decision processes encountered in automated penetration testing, the platform provides a practical basis for evaluating autonomous decision-making approaches. https://doi.org/10.3390/app16167887 Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 5 of 27 2.2.1. Network Topology and State Space Instantiation To construct global state sequences with deep causal dependencies, the simulation platform adopts a realistic enterprise architecture consisting of an external Internet segment and four internal logical security domains. The resulting topology contains ten critical business nodes, as illustrated in Figure 1. In the proposed POMDP formulation, the global state is instantiated as the collection of multi-dimensional attributes associated with all network nodes. Specifically, the states of each node is characterized by two categories of information: • System attributes: IP address, operating system, open ports, running services, and known vulnerabilities. • Acquired assets: user credentials, uploaded attack tools, and discovered sensitive files. User credentials and file assets are further categorized according to their associated entities (physical hosts or service layers) to explicitly model the prerequisite dependencies required for privilege escalation and lateral movement. The benchmark contains ten representative business nodes distributed across four logical security zones: three nodes in the DMZ, three in the Office zone, two in the Operations zone, and two in the Core Production zone. Its complexity is therefore determined not by node count alone, but by the joint effects of network segmentation, state representation, combinatorial actions, and causal dependencies. Specifically, the observation representation covers discovered hosts, vulnerabilities, credentials, files, deployed tools, and attack-failure history. The action representation contains eight parameter fields, whose unconstrained Cartesian product yields 60,374,160 raw theoretical combinations. Only a small fraction of these combinations satisfy action-template compatibility and observation-dependent prerequisites, including network reachability, credential availability, privilege, and tool deployment. Moreover, reaching the final asset in the Core Production zone requires the agent to progress from the externally exposed DMZ through multiple trust boundaries and attack stages. Therefore, although compact in node count, the environment is sufficient for the controlled evaluation of the cross-domain causal reasoning and long-horizon action-selection challenges targeted in this work. Figure 1. Network Topology. 2.2.2. Action Space Instantiation In the proposed environment, each attack action is defined as a parameterized combination a = {p1, p2, . . . , pn}, where the parameter values originate from two distinct sources. The first source consists of environment-specific information dynamically acquired by https://doi.org/10.3390/app16167887 Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 6 of 27 the agent during interaction, such as discovered IP addresses, compromised credentials, and identified exploitable vulnerabilities. The second source consists of prior knowledge stored in the agent’s static knowledge base, including commonly used attack tools and frequently observed weak-password patterns. The mapping from the 22 actionName values to their required parameter fields, operational semantics, and action categories is provided in Appendix B. In penetration testing scenarios, action validity is constrained by two factors. The first is semantic consistency among action parameters, requiring all parameter combinations within an action to satisfy logical compatibility constraints. For example, when using a directory-scanning tool (e.g., dirsearch), the action type must correspond to keyword_scan; when invoking an offline password-cracking tool (e.g., john_the_ripper), a target file (e.g., an id_rsa private key) must be specified; and when performing vulnerability exploitation, the selected [...] investigation. First, the current environment models state transitions deterministically to ensure controlled and reproducible evaluation, while future studies can investigate stochastic transition mechanisms to better characterize dynamic enterprise networks. Second, broader experimental studies with larger-scale repeated evaluations can further analyze the statistical robustness and general applicability of the proposed framework. Third, although the ten-node, four-zone benchmark is sufficient to reproduce the cross-domain causal structure targeted in this study, it does not by itself establish scalability with respect to increasing node count, topology heterogeneity, or the number of concurrent attack paths. Extending the simulation environment with more diverse network structures, vulnerability distributions, and adaptive defensive behaviors will provide additional insights into the scalability of autonomous penetration-testing agents. In addition, the current study evaluates LLM reliability primarily through taskgrounded behavioral evidence rather than an independently annotated candidate-level hallucination rate. Establishing a finer-grained evaluation protocol that distinguishes https://doi.org/10.3390/app16167887 Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 24 of 27 schema violations, unsupported parameter generation, and prerequisite inconsistencies remains an important direction for future work. 5.2. Future Directions Based on the above limitations, several directions will be investigated in future work. First, the simulation environment will be extended toward larger-scale and more dynamic enterprise networks, including stochastic state transitions and adaptive defense mechanisms, to further evaluate the robustness and generalization capability of L-ARLPT. Second, future research will explore adaptive knowledge-driven mechanisms, including parameter-efficient fine-tuning, continual learning, and retrieval-based knowledge updates, allowing LLM components to incorporate newly observed environmental information while maintaining training stability. Third, more systematic optimization strategies for reward design will be investigated, including automated reward adjustment and sensitivity analysis, to reduce dependence on manually defined reward parameters. Finally, future evaluations will incorporate more extensive statistical validation through repeated independent experiments, enabling more rigorous analysis of performance variance and significance. These improvements will further enhance the reliability and applicability of L-ARLPT in practical autonomous penetration-testing scenarios. Author Contributions: Conceptualization, R.Z. and L.W.; methodology, R.Z. and L.W.; software, R.Z.; validation, R.Z., J.Z., R.H. and Y.X.; formal analysis, R.Z. and C.C.; investigation, R.Z.; resources, R.Z. and J.Z.; data curation, R.Z. and J.Z.; writing—original draft preparation, R.Z. and J.Z.; writing—review and editing, L.W., R.Z., C.C. and J.Z.; visualization, R.Z.; supervision, R.Z.; project administration, L.W.; funding acquisition, L.W. All authors have read and agreed to the published version of the manuscript. Funding: This work is supported by the National Natural Science Foundation of China (Grant No. 62572136 and No. W2511073). Institutional Review Board Statement: Not applicable. Informed Consent Statement: Not applicable. Data Availability Statement: The original contributions presented in this study are included in the article. Further inquiries can be directed to the corresponding author. Conflicts of Interest: The authors declare no conflicts of interest. Appendix A The following additional configurations complement the hyperparameter settings summarized in Table A1 and provide the complete empirical setup used for L-ARLPT training. PER used α = 0.6, β = 0.4, and η = 10−6, with replacement sampling, maximumnormalized importance-sampling weights, and maximum-priority initialization for new transitions (1.0 when the buffer was empty). The discount factor was set to γ = 0.95, where the Huber threshold parameter δhuber was set to 1.0. The knowledge-driven layer employed Qwen3-14B for strategic planning and Qwen3-Max for action generation, both with a temperature of 0.3; their maximum output lengths were 2048 and 4096 tokens, respectively. Each request allowed three attempts, with timeout limits of 180 s and 300 s. The action-generation prompt requested K = 5 proposals. https://doi.org/10.3390/app16167887 Appl. Sci. 2026, 16, 7887 25 of 27 Table A1. DQN Algorithm Hyperparameter Settings. Method Learning Rate Discount Factor Replay Buffer Size Batch Size Target network update Exploration strategy L-ARLPT 3 × 10−4 0.95 1 × 106 512 every 100 steps ϵ-greedy Initial ϵ: 1.0 Exploration parameters details Min ϵ: 0.1 Decay factor: 0.995 BDQ 3 × 10−5 0.999 1 × 106 512 every 100 steps ϵ-greedy Initial ϵ: 1.0 Min ϵ: 0.1 Decay factor: 0.995 Wolpertinger Actor: 3 × 10−4 Critic: 3 × 10−5 0.999 1 × 106 512 Soft update τ = 0.005 Adaptive Initial ϵ: 0.1 Max ϵ: 0.9 Min ϵ: 0.1 Success trigger (high/low): 30%/5% Appendix B The actionName domain contains 22 supported actions. Each action is associated with a parameter template that specifies its required fields, together with an operational description and an action category. The templates below list only the parameters required by each action. 1. keyword_scan (networkIP, word, tool): Enumerate directories or files on a target host or service. [Probing and Scanning] 2. file_scan (networkIP): Read sensitive files on a target node to obtain credentials, configuration, or network topology. [Probing and Scanning] 3. vul_scan (networkIP, tool): Attack a specified network using a previously scanned node as a pivot. [Probing and Scanning] 4. network_scan (networkIP, tool): Collect information about a target network. [Probing and Scanning] 5. port_scan (networkIP, tool): Scan ports on a target host. [Probing and Scanning] 6. grafana_file_read (networkIP, tool): Exploit CVE-2021-43798 to read files from a Grafana server. [Exploitation] 7. ms17_010 (networkIP, tool): Exploit EternalBlue (MS17-010) to gain system privileges. [Exploitation] 8. oa_rce (networkIP): Exploit CVE-2022-22916 to compromise an O2OA server. [Ex- ploitation] 9. jenkins_rce (networkIP): Exploit CVE-2018-1000861 to compromise a Jenkins server. [Exploitation] 10. gitlab_rce (networkIP): Exploit CVE-2021-22205 to compromise a GitLab server. [Ex- ploitation] 11. weak_passwd (networkIP, userName, passwd): Attempt to log in to a target server using weak passwords. [Exploitation] 12. ifconfig (networkIP, tool): Obtain network interface information of a target node. [Command Execution] 13. upload_tool (networkIP, tool): Upload an attack tool to a target node. [Command Execution] 14. msf_hashdump (networkIP, tool): Dump NTLM hashes from a Windows system. [Command Execution] 15. john_the_ripper (networkIP, tool, phyFileName): Offline cracking of hash files or SSH keys. [Command Execution]